## Steps and evidence

### STEP 0 -- syntax, measured with `?` and NO trailing CR
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny ?
  MAC  Source host's MAC address in HHHH.HHHH.HHHH format
  any  Source any
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny 000d.b955.770f ?
  MASK  Source wildcard MAC address in HHHH.HHHH.HHHH format
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny 000d.b955.770f ffff.ffff.ffff ?
  MAC  Destination host's MAC address in HHHH.HHHH.HHHH format
  any  Destination any
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny <src> <mask> <dst> <mask> ?
  inner-vlan         inner-vlan
  tunnel-terminated  tunnel-terminated packets
  vlan               vlan
  <cr>
  Form: access-list <4000-4699> <action> <src MAC|any> [<src MASK>] <dst MAC|any> [<dst MASK>]
        [vlan <vid>] [inner-vlan <vid>] [tunnel-terminated]

### STEP 1 -- a 4000-range MAC list applied to a port denies the matching flow
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4000 deny 000d.b955.770f 0000.0000.0000 0000.cd42.3029 0000.0000.0000
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group 4000
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 4000
  Hardware MAC access list 4000
      4 deny 000d.b955.770f 0000.0000.0000 0000.cd42.3029 0000.0000.0000
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss        DENIED
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 4000
      Packet Hits: 6
  => STEP 1 PASS: a numbered hardware MAC ACL bound to a port denies traffic between the
     named source and destination MAC addresses, with the counter corroborating.

### STEP 2 -- the mask is a WILDCARD, proven from BOTH directions
The mask could be read two ways -- 1 = "must match" or 1 = "don't care". A rule whose named
MAC is the traffic's own MAC matches under EITHER reading, so that alone proves nothing. Two
independent measurements settle it.

  (a) vary the TRAFFIC, hold the rule. Frames sent from the spoofed MAC 02:00:00:00:00:99,
      which no rule names:
        list  rule                                                        hits    matched?
        4000  deny 000d.b955.770f 0000.0000.0000 0000.cd42.3029 0000...   0 -> 0  NO
        4002  deny 000d.b955.770f ffff.ffff.ffff any                      0 -> 5  YES
      4002 matched a source MAC it does not name. Under "1 = must match" that is impossible.

  (b) vary the RULE, hold the traffic. A MAC that exists nowhere on this bench, with an
      all-ones mask, against the ordinary 6-ping flow:
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4004 deny 0000.0000.0001 ffff.ffff.ffff any
        host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 4004
        Packet Hits: 9
      It matched traffic from 000d.b955.770f while naming 0000.0000.0001. Under "1 = must
      match" this rule would have matched nothing at all.

  And the converse control:
        list  rule                                        hits    result
        4001  deny 0000.0000.0001 0000.0000.0000 any      0       0% loss -- matched nothing
      A zero mask against an absent MAC correctly matches nothing, so 0 bits ARE significant.

  => STEP 2 PASS: the mask is a WILDCARD -- a 1 bit means "don't care", a 0 bit means "must
     match". This is what the AW+ ACL guide describes, so the platform agrees with the
     documentation; it is recorded because it was measured, not assumed.

### STEP 3 -- `any` forms
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4003 deny any any
  host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss ; Packet Hits: 3
  => STEP 3 PASS: `deny any any` denies all traffic ingressing the port, as expected.

### STEP 4 -- the vlan qualifier selects correctly
  list  qualifier   .65 result          expected
  4006  vlan 100    100% packet loss    DENY -- the test traffic IS in VLAN 100
  4007  vlan 1        0% packet loss    PASS -- the test traffic is not in VLAN 1
  Both lists are otherwise identical (same source MAC, same destination MAC, same masks).
  => STEP 4 PASS: the vlan qualifier narrows a MAC rule to the tagged VLAN rather than being
     ignored, proven by a matching/non-matching pair on an otherwise identical rule.

### STEP 5 -- copy-to-cpu on a MAC list: BOTH halves proven
The ACL guide defines copy-to-cpu as "copy to the CPU AND forward". Both halves must be shown;
proving only one would not distinguish it from send-to-cpu.
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 4005 copy-to-cpu 000d.b955.770f 0000.0000.0000 0000.cd42.3029 0000.0000.0000
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48 ; access-group 4005
x960-56YSQ#show platform counter sdma       (before, then after 8 pings)
              0 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]   ->      9 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]
  host# ping -c 8 .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 4005
      Packet Hits: 9
  => STEP 5 PASS: CPU receive-queue 0 advanced from 0 to 9, exactly matching the 9 ACL hits,
     so the frames were genuinely copied to the CPU; and the ping passed 0% loss, so they were
     ALSO still forwarded to the far side. That is copy-to-cpu behaving to its definition.
     (Queue 6's counter was also read; it does not accumulate the same way and is not used as
     evidence here. Queue 6 is the send-to-cpu queue and belongs to group 3's T44989.)

### STEP 6 -- D2 removal
Every 4000-range list created here was unbound after its measurement and all are removed in
the group's shared restore (R0), which is gated on `show access-list` being empty and the
classifier reading returning to the recorded baseline of 2/1024.
  => STEP 6 PASS for this case's own bindings: each `no access-group <n>` was accepted and the
     following measurement showed the traffic restored.

## Scope note -- the case's "trunk port with vlan 600"
The stored steps illustrate the feature on a trunk port carrying VLAN 600. This bench's
deterministic path is a VLAN 100 ACCESS port (port1.0.48); converting it to a trunk would have
rebuilt the path the whole group is measured against, between cases, which STANDING-ORDERS §6
forbids. The mechanism the example exercises -- a MAC rule narrowed by a VLAN qualifier -- was
tested instead on the VLAN the bench actually carries, with a matching and a non-matching
qualifier (STEP 4). The VLAN number is incidental to the behaviour; the qualifier is not.

## Findings
F1  The MAC mask is a WILDCARD: 1 = don't care, 0 = must match. Measured from both directions
    (STEP 2), not inferred from a single rule.
F2  `deny proto 1` in the IP range is canonicalised to `deny icmp` (carried from T44987); no
    equivalent canonicalisation was seen in the MAC range.
F3  A bound numbered list cannot be edited (carried from T44987) -- this is why each variant
    here is its own list rather than extra entries on one list.

VERDICT: PASS -- numbered hardware MAC ACLs in the 4000-4699 range bind to a port with
`access-group` and filter on source and destination MAC. The mask behaves as a wildcard, proven
both by a spoofed source MAC matching an all-ones-mask rule that does not name it and by an
absent MAC with an all-ones mask matching ordinary traffic, with a zero-mask control matching
nothing. `any` forms work, the vlan qualifier narrows a rule to the tagged VLAN and is ignored
for another VLAN, and copy-to-cpu copies matching frames to CPU queue 0 while still forwarding
them, with the CPU counter and the ACL counter agreeing exactly.
